# Pixi vs React

## Pixi: draws the board, nothing else
- Map tiles, walls, wall fade, darkness, animated tiles: `src/components/maps/pixi/mapLayers.js`, `pixiStage.js`.
- Character and NPC figures: `src/components/maps/pixi/figures.js`.
- Challenge-object outlines and markers, last-known markers, click pulses: `src/components/exploration/pixi/boardItems.js`.
- Effects (transporter beams, scan sweeps, shots, impacts, strikes, floating labels, the line of fire): `src/components/maps/pixi/effects.js`, built for combat by `src/components/combat/actionEffects.js`.
- The map editor's markers, room labels and tile previews: `src/components/maps/pixi/editorMarkers.js`.
- Per-board scenes: `exploration/pixi/boardScene.js`, `combat/pixi/battleScene.js`, `maps/pixi/mapScene.js`.
- Pixi code reads the current board each frame (`stage.onFrame`) and never owns or changes game state.

## React: everything else
- All game state: reducers, world clock, combat state, character data.
- HUD, sidebars, menus, dialogs, Settings, Captain's Log screens.
- The board components (`PixiExplorationBoard`, `PixiBattlefield`, `EditorBoard`): props, pointer input, click matching via `src/maps/boardHitTest.js` (not Pixi events).
- Tile thumbnails outside the board (the editor palette, `TileThumbnail.jsx`) are canvases drawn by `canvasTiles.js`.

## SVG: UI only
- Pixi is the only board renderer; there is no SVG board and no `?board=svg`. If WebGL won't start, the board shows `WebglUnavailable` instead.
- SVG is used only where it controls the UI or gives the game a function: the invisible overlay that sets the camera (Pixi follows its viewBox) and takes pointer input, `UnitActionRing` menus, `<title>` tooltips, the drag-select box, the editor's hover outlines, the minimaps, and the debug overlays.
- Anything drawn on the board goes in Pixi, not SVG. Tell the designer before adding any new SVG.

## Constraints
- Game rules and calculations go in the rules layer, never in Pixi scenes or board components.
- Choosing how a new visual is split between Pixi and the overlay is an implementation detail; changing what the player sees is the designer's call.
